SHAMSU — CLI presentation layer (Track A): rich the renderer
============================================================
2026-08-21 · branch small-shamsu-new
Model for the live runs: qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct (per CLAUDE.md §5)


WHAT THIS IS NOT
----------------
No `rich.Live` layout, no pinned input box, no sidebar. `simple_feedback.py`
runs a raw `msvcrt` keystroke thread during a turn so the user can steer
mid-run; a display that owns and repaints the screen from another thread fights
it, and on Windows that is the fight you lose. `console.status` coexists
because it is transient and unpinned. That was a deliberate scope decision
(Track A), not an omission.


WHAT WAS ALREADY THERE, AND WAS NOT REBUILT
-------------------------------------------
- `rich` is used in 12 modules already; it is not a new dependency.
- A `dots` spinner already ran during every turn (`repl.py`,
  `console.status(..., spinner="dots")`).
- `shamsu/runtime/turn_stream.py` is a real event bus: 9 kinds (now 10), `text`
  plus structured `data`, monotonic `seq`, a bounded queue whose drop policy
  never sheds a `tool.call`, three renderers, and a parity test.
- `shamsu/cli/turn_render.py` already existed as the CLI's renderer over it.

So this was not "build a presentation layer". The layer existed and painted
everything the same:

    self.console.print(f"[dim]{event.text}[/dim]")

One grey for a successful read and for a failed `run_tests` alike.


THE ARCHITECTURE CALL
---------------------
The brief asked the terminal to render from `ActionLedger`. It renders from the
turn stream instead, for three reasons:

1. The ledger is a disk writer and every call into it is swallowed
   (`try: ... except Exception: pass`). Correct for logging, poison for a UI:
   a rendering bug becomes a silently blank screen.
2. The ledger has no `status` events, so the spinner would have no source.
3. CLI/Telegram parity is asserted against the stream; rendering from a
   different bus makes "every line reaches every surface" unprovable.

The INTENT behind the request - the terminal and `log-summary.md` must not
disagree - is kept and made testable instead:
`test_the_terminal_the_phone_and_the_session_log_agree` runs one turn and
asserts all three surfaces report the same actions in the same order.


EMITS ADDED (you cannot render what nothing emits)
--------------------------------------------------
`shamsu/agents/simple_chat.py`, additive only - no loop logic changed:

    status        + ctx_pct, ctx_text, round, max_rounds   (every tick)
    status        emitted immediately at tool start and after each model call
    tool.result   + duration_ms, target, diff
    reasoning     NEW kind - the thinking channel reached the ledger but never
                  the stream, so no surface could show it
    approval      NEW emission - the loop never published one at all. The
                  question went straight to its own Console from inside a tool,
                  so every surface watching saw a gap where a human was being
                  asked something. On the terminal that is precisely why the
                  prompt and the spinner used to fight.

`reasoning` and `approval` are NOT body kinds: a trace belongs to the response
that produced it and an approval to the write it gated, so neither is a
separate ACTION. The CLI renders both by reading `data`, which is what the
module docstring says `data` is for. Parity is therefore untouched.


THE RENDER, LIVE
----------------
Real turn, real model, real console:

    shamsu (renderlive2) cli> divide() in calc.py crashes on a zero divisor.

      model responded in 3s
      ▤ Reading calc.py · 18ms
      model responded in 1s
      ▤ Reading symbol calc.py · 15ms
      model responded in 2s
      ✎ Editing calc.py · 129ms
        +    if b == 0:
        +        raise ValueError('Cannot divide by zero')
      ✗ run_tests FAILED I could not work out how to run tests here... · 13ms
      model responded in 2s

     ✓ SUCCESS  done in 10s - 1 file changed

...and the spinner, frame by frame:

    ⠋ thinking...          | ctx 10% (3.3k/32.8k) | rnd 1/24
    ⠋ read_file calc.py    | ctx 10% (3.3k/32.8k) | rnd 1/24
    ⠋ thinking...          | ctx 10% (3.4k/32.8k) | rnd 2/24
    ⠋ read_symbol calc.py  | ctx 10% (3.4k/32.8k) | rnd 2/24
    ⠋ patch_file calc.py   | ctx 11% (3.5k/32.8k) | rnd 3/24
    ⠋ run_tests            | ctx 11% (3.5k/32.8k) | rnd 3/24
    ⠋ thinking...          | ctx 12% (4.0k/32.8k) | rnd 4/24

The meter goes yellow at 60% and red at 80%. Both numbers existed already
(`SESSION_COUNTERS.meter()`, the round index) and were displayed nowhere - you
found out you had filled the window by watching the run degrade.


FIVE DEFECTS THE FIRST RENDERED PREVIEW CAUGHT
----------------------------------------------
None of these were visible in a unit test. They were visible the moment a turn
was painted to a real console.

1. THE EDIT ROW PRINTED TWICE. `patch_file` asks for approval from INSIDE the
   call that is still running, and the approval handler flushed the pending
   row - so the edit appeared once unfinished at 0ms and again when the result
   came back. Approvals no longer flush.
2. A COLLAPSED ROW CLAIMED ONE CALL'S DURATION FOR ALL OF THEM.
   `contract_status x8 · 6ms` - the 6ms was the last call's.
3. FAILURE READ AFTER THE DURATION. How long a failure took is the least
   interesting thing about it.
4. DIFF FURNITURE WAS SHOWN AS CHANGES. `---`/`+++`/`@@` name the file you are
   already looking at and cost three of the eight lines on offer.
5. THE COLLAPSE RULE WOULD NEVER HAVE FIRED. Written to collapse consecutive
   identical calls - but the run it was written for, live 2026-08-21, has a
   "model responded" line between every one of the eight `contract_status`
   calls. A repeat is now the same tool as the last row PAINTED, not the last
   event seen, and the model's replies inside a run are held too. Otherwise
   eight suppressed rows still cost eight lines.

Sixteen events, three lines:

      ▤ contract_status · 6ms
      model responded in 1s
      ▤ contract_status x8

Deliberately not an in-place rewrite of a printed line: `console.status` pins a
live region below the cursor, so the last line on screen is the spinner, not
the row - cursor-relative editing would corrupt it. The count climbs on the
spinner instead, which is already a live element and already safe.


TESTS
-----
    tests/test_turn_stream_parity.py    28 -> 44

New, worth naming:

    test_the_terminal_the_phone_and_the_session_log_agree
        three-way parity. One turn, one loop; the bus, the card and
        log-summary.md must report the same actions in the same order.
    test_a_thrash_collapses_across_the_model_lines_between_it
        the shape a real contract loop actually has. 16 events, <=4 lines, and
        all 16 still on the parity list.
    test_an_approval_does_not_print_its_tool_row_twice
    test_a_collapsed_row_does_not_claim_one_calls_duration_for_all_of_them
    test_a_failure_reads_before_its_duration
    test_diff_headers_are_not_shown_as_changes
    test_the_spinner_carries_the_context_meter_and_the_round
    test_an_unfinished_call_is_still_printed
        a turn that dies mid-tool must not swallow the row for the tool that
        was running when it died - that is the one you want to see.

Two existing assertions changed, both because they encoded the old painting:
`assert line in printed` became a check on the line's SUBJECT (a row is now an
icon, a verb, a target and a duration), and `assert "done in 3s" not in
printed` became `assert "SUCCESS" in printed` - the verdict was previously
dropped entirely, so a turn ended by scrolling off with no statement of
whether it had worked.


NOT DONE IN THIS COMMIT
-----------------------
Folding `.shamsu/chat-logs/` into `log-detailed.md`. It is 20 call sites inside
the agent loop plus a module and ten tests, which deserves its own diff and its
own suite run rather than riding along with a UI change.

While scoping it: `shamsu/agents/simple_log.py` contains ZERO calls to
`redact`. Every other path that writes a prompt or a response to disk goes
through the single secret-pattern enforcement point; that one does not. So
`.shamsu/chat-logs/*.md` holds unredacted prompts and model output. That is
the more important half of the item and is the reason to do it properly.
